/* Generated from TSAR_master_verification_2509.xlsx (sheet TSAR_data, column Bottlenecks_explained). Used by the method detail page. */
window.NAM_BOTTLENECK_EXPLANATIONS = {
"TM2012-06": "No documented bottleneck identified.",
"TM2020-01": "Validation evidence limitations were identified during peer review, including a protracted validation programme with changes in participating laboratories, limited transferability and within-laboratory reproducibility data, and a relatively small chemical test set. These limitations created uncertainty around transferability and the applicability domain, although ESAC ultimately considered reproducibility, transferability and predictive capacity adequate for the defined regulatory purpose.",
"TM2020-04": "Validation evidence limitations included a non-a-priori validation design, limited numbers of chemicals and laboratory replicates, suboptimal within- and between-laboratory reproducibility, and insufficient evidence for chemicals requiring metabolic activation. These limitations restricted confidence in parts of the applicability domain and support the use of complementary evidence or combined testing with the RS Comet assay, although ESAC considered the method sufficiently valid for its defined purpose.",
"TM2009-14": "Validation and regulatory development encountered evidence-readiness and applicability-domain limitations. ESAC concluded in 2014 that transferability and reproducibility were adequate, but predictive capacity was only partially established and the available evidence was insufficient to conclude regulatory readiness, particularly because of the small, pharmaceutical-focused chemical set and limited coverage of challenging chemicals. The OECD guideline project was subsequently suspended in 2020 and resumed in 2021 with additional chemical-augmentation work. These limitations were sufficiently resolved for adoption of OECD TG 445A in July 2026, although endpoint-specific uncertainty, particularly for CYP2B6, and defined applicability limitations remain.",
"TM2022-03": "No major validation or standardisation bottleneck was identified. Peer review nevertheless documented applicability and integration limitations: α-Sens® is not intended as a stand-alone skin sensitisation method, aqueous test conditions can restrict testing of poorly soluble substances, and the cell system has limited metabolic capacity. These limitations support its use as one source of information within a Defined Approach or IATA rather than as an independent hazard assessment.",
"TM2026-01": "Earlier validation work identified a regulatory-performance mismatch: the Lung Surfactant Bioassay did not predict GHS acute inhalation toxicity classifications based on conventional animal-study outcomes, although it successfully predicted respiratory clinical signs associated with decreased lung function. Because the assay captures surfactant-dependent effects only, it is not sufficient as a stand-alone acute inhalation toxicity method and requires integration with complementary evidence. This represents a documented predictive-performance, applicability-domain and regulatory-integration bottleneck rather than a mechanistic-relevance problem.",
"TM2025-02": "[Validation/readiness] EURL ECVAM considered the submitted presubmission package insufficient to demonstrate readiness for formal validation. Later partially addressed by the submitter through a revised presubmission and external multi-center prevalidation trial (Urbančič et al., 2026; 49 instillation materials, 7 blind OECD TG 412 inhalation substances). Status: partially resolved. [Mechanistic relevance] EURL ECVAM and PARERE requested further justification of the biological and mechanistic relevance of the murine LA-4/MH-S co-culture subcellular dynamics and 17 time-dependent observables in representing long-term human pulmonary inflammation. Status: partially resolved. [Regulatory relevance/integration] PARERE consultation (March–May 2026) revealed regulatory uncertainty regarding how the in-vitro-learned digital twin LOAEL predictions can be integrated into OECD hazard classification frameworks (TGs 403, 412, 413, 452). Status: active. [Programme prioritisation/route selection] EURL ECVAM evaluation stopped the presubmission track in May 2026 (SUB_STOP) following PARERE feedback, advising submitters to build additional regulatory confidence prior to resubmission. Status: active.",
"TM2019-06": "Within-laboratory validation showed good-to-excellent reproducibility, very good within-run variability and generally good sensitivity and specificity. The remaining validation bottleneck identified by the OECD Thyroid Disruption Methods Expert Group was transferability: between-laboratory reproducibility had not yet been demonstrated because the assay had been evaluated in a single laboratory. Further transferability testing in one or more independent laboratories was therefore recommended, and PEPPER initiated a follow-up validation study in 2026.",
"TM2017-02": "EURL ECVAM considered the principle and biological and general mechanistic relevance of TR MARCoNI sufficiently described to invite a full submission, but identified remaining evidence questions concerning method reproducibility, its added regulatory value relative to existing thyroid assays, and additional evidence from positive compounds; potential intellectual-property issues were also flagged for clarification. A full test submission was subsequently not received, and the TSAR process was stopped in April 2022. These events document evidence-readiness, reproducibility/transferability, regulatory-integration and programme/route-selection bottlenecks. No specific predictive-performance failure, mechanistic-relevance deficiency or standardisation/adoption barrier was documented.",
"TM2016-01": "EURL ECVAM considered the principle, biological relevance and general mechanistic relevance of EDITOX sufficiently described, but PARERE/EURL ECVAM identified limitations in its intended stand-alone regulatory application. Because EDITOX addresses a single mode of action based on 5-HT2CR RNA editing, it was not expected to fully replace in vivo testing for depression or pro-suicidal effects on its own. The submitter was asked to address other relevant modes of action and possible complementary in vitro methods within a testing battery in a full submission. The requested full submission was subsequently not received, and the TSAR process was stopped in April 2019. These events document evidence-readiness, applicability-domain, regulatory-integration and programme/route-selection bottlenecks. No specific failure of predictive performance, mechanistic relevance, reproducibility or standardisation was documented.",
"TM2016-03": "EURL ECVAM and PARERE considered ToxTracker biologically and mechanistically relevant and sufficiently promising to invite a full submission. EURL ECVAM requested that the full submission be completed once validation-study data became available. No full submission was subsequently received and the TSAR route was stopped in April 2019. In parallel, ToxTracker progressed through an OECD-led route, including an international multi-laboratory validation study, independent peer review in 2023 and eventual adoption as OECD TG 446A in July 2026. The documented bottlenecks therefore concern evidence-package timing/readiness and programme/route selection rather than reproducibility, predictive performance, mechanistic relevance or regulatory relevance.",
"TM2019-18": "The original thyroid-validation route was stopped because the original human neural progenitor/stem-cell system raised ethical and source-access concerns and the alternative hiPSC-based test system required further standardisation. Subsequent EURL ECVAM-sponsored work using commercially available hiPSC-derived BrainSpheres progressed into validation-oriented testing, but identified substantial between-experiment variability, operator dependence of the imaging readout, inconsistent responses to reference compounds and an inability to establish final acceptance criteria. Alternative quantitative approaches, including flow cytometry, are being developed and the assay is undergoing further validation-oriented work for potential inclusion in the developmental neurotoxicity in vitro battery. These events document validation-readiness, reproducibility/transferability, predictive-performance, access/implementation, standardisation and programme/route-selection bottlenecks.",
"TM2019-16": "The OECD Thyroid Disruption Methods Expert Group considered ZETA promising but not yet adequately validated for Test Guideline development. Across-run reproducibility was only fair and substantial variability was observed for several chemicals. Specificity remained uncertain because expected negative chemicals produced apparent activity at high concentrations, highlighting the need to better define maximum tolerated concentrations and distinguish thyroid-specific responses from systemic toxicity. Further work was also requested on embryo numbers, concentration and solvent harmonisation, statistical interpretation across runs and antibody standardisation. These findings document validation-readiness, reproducibility/transferability, predictive-performance, applicability-domain and standardisation/adoption bottlenecks. No mechanistic-relevance, regulatory-integration or programme-route bottleneck was identified.",
"TM2019-03": "OECD TDM-EG assessment identified substantial validation limitations for the TSHR-cAMP assay. Within-run variability was generally acceptable, but between-run variability was high and the assay's dynamic range varied markedly between experiments. All chemicals tested in the validation set produced negative results, preventing adequate assessment of sensitivity, specificity, activity thresholds and routine-test performance. The expert group concluded that the available data were insufficient to determine validation status and recommended identifying suitable positive reference chemicals, optimising the agonist protocol and developing an antagonist mode. These findings document validation-readiness, reproducibility/transferability, predictive-performance, applicability-domain and standardisation/adoption bottlenecks.",
"TM2019-10": "The DIO1-SK assay demonstrated satisfactory within-laboratory reproducibility and mechanistic performance, and blinded testing supported its ability to identify DIO1 inhibitors. Remaining validation needs concern transferability and between-laboratory reproducibility, leading to further validation by PEPPER. A method-specific applicability limitation is also documented because iodine-containing or otherwise interfering substances can affect the Sandell-Kolthoff readout independently of DIO1 activity, requiring dedicated interference controls or orthogonal LC-MS/MS confirmation. The assay has subsequently entered the OECD Test Guidelines Programme as Project 4.179 and is undergoing development toward a new Test Guideline. These events document validation-readiness, transferability, applicability-domain and standardisation/adoption bottlenecks rather than a failure of predictive or mechanistic performance.",
"TM2019-11": "The GLUC-LCMS assay showed good within-laboratory reproducibility and generally performed as designed, but its validation package remains incomplete. Additional negative chemicals are required to establish specificity, and transfer to at least one independent laboratory is needed to demonstrate between-laboratory reproducibility. The OECD Thyroid Disruption Methods Expert Group also questioned the physiological relevance of an assay focused on inhibition of thyroid-hormone glucuronidation, because increased glucuronidation and consequent thyroid-hormone clearance is a more commonly observed adverse mechanism in vivo. Additional understanding of the UGT enzymes and metabolites contributing to individual responses was recommended. These findings document validation-readiness, transferability, predictive-performance, mechanistic-relevance, programme-route and standardisation/adoption bottlenecks.",
"TM2019-19": "Part 1 of the EURL ECVAM validation assessed method definition, robustness, reliability and within-laboratory reproducibility. The planned Part 2 study, intended to assess thyroid-mechanistic relevance using a broader validation chemical set, was not performed because reductions in human resources at the participating EU-NETVAL laboratory prevented continuation of the validation programme. Validation was subsequently stopped in February 2023. The resulting evidence gap therefore reflects incomplete validation readiness and programme/resource constraints rather than demonstrated failure of reproducibility, predictive performance or mechanistic relevance.",
"TM2019-17": "EURL ECVAM validation and subsequent OECD TDM-EG assessment showed that T-SCREEN can reproducibly detect thyroid-receptor agonist activity, and the expert group considered the assay valid for detection of TR agonists. However, the antagonist mode remained insufficiently optimised and produced ambiguous results. The assay also lacks thyroid specificity because its endpoint is GH3-cell proliferation, which can be influenced by hormones, growth factors and other non-thyroid pathways. Further work was recommended to characterise specificity, quality and acceptance criteria, cell-line stability and between-laboratory reproducibility before consideration for Test Guideline development. These findings document validation-readiness, transferability, predictive-performance, mechanistic-relevance, regulatory-integration and standardisation/adoption bottlenecks.",
"TM2019-15": "Validation of TRβ-CALUX demonstrated generally good performance for agonist detection, but the antagonist component remains less mature. Differences between laboratories and incomplete antagonist data raise transferability concerns, while additional active agonist and antagonist chemicals are needed to better characterise assay performance. OECD assessment also identified assay-specific interference because some chemicals can stabilise or destabilise luciferase, potentially causing misleading reporter responses and requiring explicit applicability-domain and viability criteria in data interpretation. Further between-laboratory validation and refinement of interpretation criteria would be required before Test Guideline development. Programme progression also remains conditional on regulatory need and the added utility of the assay relative to alternative approaches such as in-silico models.",
"TM2019-14": "EURL ECVAM validation and subsequent OECD TDM-EG assessment demonstrated that the TRα/TRβ reporter assays are valid for detecting TRα and TRβ agonists, with good specificity in the available chemical set. Reproducibility was somewhat stronger for TRβ than TRα, but the lower TRα reproducibility was not considered a barrier to assay validity. Remaining needs primarily concern demonstration of between-laboratory reproducibility before potential Test Guideline development and evaluation of whether extending the method to antagonistic activity would add regulatory utility. OECD also noted that alternative approaches, including in-silico identification of structures likely to bind the TR pocket, should be considered when determining the regulatory added value of further standardisation. These findings document validation-readiness, transferability, programme/route-selection and standardisation/adoption bottlenecks rather than deficiencies in predictive or mechanistic performance.",
"TM2019-13": "The original EURL ECVAM validation pathway for MCT8-TR did not document a scientific performance failure. Instead, method definition was stopped because the drafted SOP was not provided, while both the Part 1 robustness/reproducibility study and Part 2 mechanistic-relevance study were reportedly performed but their study reports were not provided. Consequently, the validation evidence package could not be assessed or progressed within the TSAR route. Subsequent scientific work demonstrated continued development and application of the Sandell-Kolthoff-based MCT8 uptake assay, and in June 2026 BfR initiated the ThyroTransVal project for formal validation of an MCT8-SK assay using human-MCT8-transfected MDCK1 cells. These events document validation-readiness, programme/route, evidence-access/implementation and standardisation/adoption bottlenecks rather than demonstrated failure of assay performance or mechanistic relevance.",
"TM2019-12": "The THSUL-LC validation pathway was stopped during method definition before experimental validation could begin. An EU-NETVAL laboratory had been tasked with developing a test method from available published approaches, but completion of the method definition and description was discontinued in January 2022 because further method development was required. Consequently, no method-specific robustness, reproducibility or predictive-performance failure was demonstrated; these characteristics were not sufficiently assessed. The documented bottlenecks therefore concern validation readiness, programme progression and insufficient standardisation of the method rather than demonstrated failure of the underlying thyroid-sulfation mechanism.",
"TM2019-09": "EURL ECVAM validation demonstrated good-to-excellent reproducibility of the FITC-T4 TTR competitive-binding assay, and OECD expert assessment considered the method sufficiently mature to proceed to transferability testing. The principal remaining method-specific limitation concerns interference with the fluorescence readout, including autofluorescence, quenching and related interpretation criteria, which required further refinement in the SOP. Between-laboratory transferability and blinded validation were subsequently undertaken by PEPPER. The assay entered the OECD Test Guidelines Programme as Project 4.180 and is now progressing through validation-report and draft-Test-Guideline review. These events document validation-readiness, transferability, applicability-domain and standardisation/adoption bottlenecks rather than deficient predictive or mechanistic performance.",
"TM2019-08": "EURL ECVAM validation showed substantially different performance for the two components of ANSA-FD. The TTR assay demonstrated good reproducibility and generally consistent results, whereas the TBG assay showed fair-to-poor reproducibility with multiple invalid or inconsistent experiments. OECD assessment therefore recommended further refinement of the fluorescence-interference criteria, additional assessment of the TBG component and subsequent transfer to other laboratories to establish between-laboratory reproducibility. Later work further optimised TTR-binding methodology and developed an alternative fluorescence-polarisation approach for TBG. These findings document validation-readiness, reproducibility/transferability, applicability-domain and standardisation/adoption bottlenecks rather than deficient mechanistic relevance.",
"TM2019-07": "The original NIS-SKR EURL ECVAM validation route was stopped after the Part 1 experimental study because the study report was not provided and the Mel624 cell system specified in the SOP was no longer commercially available. These events do not document failure of assay reproducibility or mechanistic performance, but instead represent evidence-access and test-system availability barriers that prevented continuation of formal validation. Subsequent work has continued development of non-radioactive Sandell-Kolthoff NIS uptake assays using alternative human NIS-expressing cell systems. Since 2024, the ThyreoSafe project has explicitly undertaken redevelopment and validation of NIS assays, including measures to ensure long-term availability of the test systems. These events document validation-readiness, programme/route, access/implementation and standardisation/adoption bottlenecks and support renewed external validation progression.",
"TM2019-04": "EURL ECVAM validation identified substantial run-to-run and baseline variability in AUR-TPO, particularly for inactive chemicals and the reference/control responses. Clear TPO inhibitors were more reproducible, but variable low-response baselines could obscure inhibition and compromise interpretation of weak activity. The OECD TDM Expert Group therefore concluded that the assay required further optimisation of the SOP and test-system handling before transfer to additional laboratories. Subsequent high-throughput human AUR-TPO work demonstrated continued method development and improved screening performance, while the ongoing ThyreoSafe programme is further developing and validating thyroperoxidase-activity assays. These events document validation-readiness, reproducibility/transferability, predictive-performance, programme/route and standardisation/adoption bottlenecks rather than deficient mechanistic relevance.",
"TM2019-02": "The TRH-CHOK1 validation pathway did not stop because of demonstrated assay-performance limitations. The participating EU-NETVAL laboratory was unable to conduct the planned Part 1 and Part 2 experimental studies because personnel were redeployed for pandemic-related emergency medical services for an extended period. Consequently, robustness, reproducibility, predictive performance and applicability domain were not adequately assessed and should not be coded as failed. The documented bottlenecks concern incomplete validation readiness, programme discontinuation, implementation capacity and failure to progress to a standardised validation package. No later formal validation or regulatory-development route for the exact TRH-CHOK1 method was identified.",
"TM2025-01": "EURL ECVAM assessment of the GARDskin Dose-Response full submission identified the need for additional validation evidence before further lifecycle progression. In particular, between-laboratory reproducibility of the dose-response implementation remains to be demonstrated and additional GHS subcategory 1A chemicals should be tested to strengthen characterisation of potency-prediction performance. These requirements are specific to the dose-response extension and cannot be considered resolved solely by the established validation of the parent GARDskin hazard assay. Subsequent studies provide substantial support for quantitative potency and point-of-departure prediction, but do not yet replace formal independent transferability validation. The documented bottlenecks therefore concern validation readiness, reproducibility/transferability and predictive-performance evidence rather than mechanistic relevance or programme discontinuation.",
"TM2024-01": "The BBB-oC pre-submission was assessed by EURL ECVAM but was not considered for follow-up validation in March 2025. The submitter was encouraged to consider recommendations made in the assessment report. Public TSAR information does not specify which technical or performance limitations drove this decision, and the available evidence therefore does not support attributing the outcome to reproducibility, predictive-performance, applicability-domain or mechanistic-relevance failure. The documented lifecycle bottlenecks are conservatively limited to validation readiness and programme/route selection: the method did not progress from pre-submission assessment into formal validation.",
"TM2016-02": "Independent ESAC peer review concluded that the Bioelution method was scientifically valid, biologically relevant for its intended comparative use, reproducible and sufficiently robust to progress toward OECD Test Guideline development. The subsequent OECD standardisation project nevertheless encountered unresolved questions concerning protocol conditions, applicability and the interpretation of relative bioaccessibility data for regulatory classification and read-across. Later scientific debate questioned the equivalence and biological relevance of gastric extraction conditions used in parts of the supporting evidence. The OECD project was stopped in 2024 and moved to Annex 1 of the Test Guidelines Programme work plan. These events therefore document validation-readiness, applicability-domain, regulatory-integration, programme/route and standardisation/adoption bottlenecks rather than failure of reproducibility.",
"TM2018-01": "EpiSensA successfully completed validation, independent peer review and international regulatory standardisation and was adopted into OECD TG 442D. Validation demonstrated good within- and between-laboratory reproducibility and acceptable predictive performance for identifying skin sensitisers. No unresolved validation, performance or regulatory-adoption bottleneck is therefore identified. A practical implementation limitation remained because the original LabCyte EPI-MODEL24 reconstructed human epidermis model had limited availability outside Japan. OECD subsequently developed Performance Standards to enable validation of comparable RhE implementations, and Epi2SensA using an alternative RhE system was later incorporated into TG 442D. This represents an access/implementation limitation of the original EpiSensA test system rather than a failure of the validated method.",
"TM2020-02": "The 22Rv1/MMTV_GR-KO assay successfully completed validation and demonstrated strong inter-laboratory reproducibility and reliable detection of androgen-receptor agonists and antagonists, leading to incorporation into OECD TG 458. No unresolved validation, predictive-performance or standardisation bottleneck is identified. The principal remaining limitation concerns applicability domain: validation focused on monoconstituent chemicals, the cell system has limited metabolic activity, and chemical-dependent interference with the luciferase/luminescence readout may require consideration. These are applicability limitations of an otherwise validated and internationally standardised assay.",
"TM2016-09": "Access/implementation barrier: OECD TG 455, corrected on 2 July 2026, states that the VM7Luc ER TA assay is currently not implementable because the VM7Luc4E2 cell line is not available from the referenced source; actions are underway to remediate this situation. This is a post-adoption implementation barrier and does not reverse regulatory adoption under OECD TG 455. Status: unresolved as of the 2026 OECD correction.",
"TM2011-09": "GARD experienced a documented interruption in its regulatory-evaluation pathway despite ultimately demonstrating good predictive performance and inter-laboratory reproducibility. Evaluation was paused because access to the proprietary web-based data-analysis application and prediction-model source code had to be arranged, the acceptability of provider-controlled prediction software under GLP and regulatory conditions was unresolved, and patent protection raised concerns regarding availability and development of similar methods. These issues required resolution through agreements with the developer and OECD-level discussions on protected method elements and regulatory implementation. Evaluation subsequently resumed, external validation was accepted, ESAC peer review was completed and GARDskin was adopted into OECD TG 442E. The documented bottlenecks therefore concern validation readiness, regulatory integration, programme/route selection, access/implementation and standardisation rather than assay reproducibility or predictive performance.",
"TM2011-11": "The original EURL ECVAM/ESAC pathway for SENS-IS was interrupted because the available evidence was considered insufficient to support a transparent and reliable peer review. Earlier assessment had already identified the need for additional information on reproducibility and predictive capacity. This did not end development of the method: subsequent external validation generated a consolidated validation report and independent peer review in 2024, followed by performance-based validation of an alternative reconstructed epidermis model in 2025. The method subsequently progressed into OECD Test Guideline development and public commenting. These events document validation-readiness, reproducibility/transferability, predictive-performance, programme/route and standardisation/adoption bottlenecks, while also demonstrating successful progression through an alternative external route.",
"TM2010-07": "No documented bottleneck identified.",
"TM2022-01": "ProtReact was not prioritised for EURL ECVAM follow-up validation after assessment of the pre-submission. EURL ECVAM considered the assay mechanistically relevant and potentially simpler and more cost-effective than existing peptide-reactivity methods, but concluded that additional evidence was required on within- and between-laboratory reproducibility and on predictive capacity. Programme prioritisation was also affected by the availability of established methods addressing the same molecular initiating event, limiting the perceived added value of another similar assay. Subsequent scientific work further characterised ProtReact using a 106-chemical dataset and demonstrated predictive performance broadly comparable to DPRA, but no later formal validation or regulatory-standardisation progression was identified. The documented bottlenecks therefore concern validation readiness, reproducibility/transferability, predictive-performance evidence and programme prioritisation rather than mechanistic relevance.",
"TM2021-01": "LUCS-VALITOX was not progressed to EURL ECVAM validation following assessment of the pre-submission. This decision was not attributed to demonstrated failures in reproducibility, predictive performance or mechanistic relevance. EURL ECVAM noted that basal cytotoxicity assays are generally interpreted together with other readouts and that similar approaches were already available, limiting the added value of prioritising another standalone cytotoxicity method. The submitter was instead encouraged to consider direct engagement with OECD for the proposed acute-oral-toxicity starting-dose application. The documented bottlenecks therefore concern regulatory integration and programme prioritisation/route selection rather than scientific-performance failure.",
"TM1994-01": "The 3T3 NRU phototoxicity assay is internationally validated and regulatorily adopted, but its applicability is limited by the physicochemical and mechanistic requirements of the test system. Reliable assessment depends on adequate light absorption, solubility, stability and achievement of suitable test concentrations under the assay conditions. The method primarily detects direct phototoxicity and is not designed to address indirect mechanisms, metabolite-mediated effects, photoallergy, photogenotoxicity or photocarcinogenicity. In addition, the predictive performance of complex mixtures and some difficult-to-test formulations is not fully characterised within the validated applicability domain. These limitations represent active applicability-domain, mechanistic-relevance and product/matrix-qualification constraints rather than unresolved validation, reproducibility or regulatory-adoption bottlenecks. The method has achieved international regulatory acceptance as OECD TG 432, with later guideline updates refining its standardised use.",
"TM1997-02": "EpiDerm-PT is a validated reconstructed human epidermis phototoxicity method and is the validated reference method underlying OECD TG 498. The remaining limitations are primarily applicability- and matrix-related rather than failures of validation, reproducibility or regulatory acceptance. The method is intended for direct phototoxicity after topical exposure and does not address endpoints such as photoallergy, photogenotoxicity, photocarcinogenicity, indirect mechanisms or metabolite-mediated effects. Evidence for some mixtures, unstable or difficult-to-test substances and materials outside the defined applicability domain remains limited. These constraints are managed through defined use conditions and, where appropriate, tiered or integrated testing strategies.",
"TM2003-03": "BCOP is internationally standardised and regulatorily adopted, but active limitations remain within its validated decision domain. Predictive performance is incomplete across the full eye-irritation spectrum: the method can identify chemicals causing serious eye damage (UN GHS Category 1) and chemicals not requiring classification, but it does not provide definitive stand-alone classification for intermediate Category 2/2A/2B chemicals. Applicability is also chemical- and physical-form dependent, with documented limitations and overprediction/underprediction concerns for some classes and difficult-to-test materials. Performance for certain formulated products, including some agrochemical formulations, is not uniform and requires matrix-specific interpretation. These limitations are managed through defined applicability criteria, optional histopathology and tiered or integrated testing strategies rather than representing unresolved validation or regulatory-adoption failure.",
"TM2003-02": "[Predictive performance] ICE does not provide definitive classification across the complete spectrum of eye irritation and serious eye damage. The method is validated to identify chemicals causing serious eye damage and chemicals not requiring classification, but intermediate eye-irritation categories cannot be comprehensively classified by ICE alone. Consequently, ICE is used within a tiered or integrated testing strategy rather than as a stand-alone substitute for the in vivo rabbit eye test across the entire hazard spectrum. Optional histopathology has subsequently been incorporated to improve predictivity for serious eye damage. Status: active; partly mitigated through tiered interpretation and histopathology. [Applicability domain] Validation studies identified chemical-class and physical-form dependent performance limitations. Historical analyses showed elevated false-positive or false-negative rates for particular classes, including alcohols, surfactants and some solid chemicals, indicating that performance was not uniform across the full chemical domain. These limitations influenced the regulatory positioning and interpretation of ICE and contributed to the definition of its validated applicability and decision framework. Status: active but managed through defined applicability criteria, updated guidance and tiered interpretation.",
"TM2008-15": "[Validation/readiness evidence package] During the initial ICCVAM assessment, the Background Review Document submitted for the STE test was considered insufficiently complete and additional information was requested from the sponsor. Several revisions followed before the final BRD was completed in 2012. Subsequent validation review and external peer review concluded that the available database was generally sufficient, enabling submission to the OECD and eventual adoption as TG 491. Status: resolved. [Predictive performance] The STE test does not provide definitive classification across the full spectrum of serious eye damage and eye irritation. In particular, it cannot identify UN GHS Category 2/2A/2B chemicals reliably, and chemicals falling outside the defined Category 1 or No Category decision regions require additional testing. Consequently, STE is intended for use within a tiered or integrated testing strategy rather than as a stand-alone replacement for the in vivo rabbit eye test across all hazard categories. Status: active; managed through tiered testing. [Applicability domain] The applicability of STE is limited for certain difficult-to-test chemicals. Solid chemicals other than defined surfactant applications, substances or mixtures that cannot be adequately dissolved or uniformly suspended in the permitted solvents, and some volatile or otherwise problematic chemicals can fall outside the validated applicability domain or produce an increased risk of false-negative results. Potential interference with the MTT read-out also requires specific consideration. Status: active, partly mitigated by defined solvent procedures, alternative read-outs and updated guidance.",
"TM2008-16": "[Validation/readiness evidence package] In 2009, ICCVAM concluded that the available evidence was insufficient to demonstrate that the proposed BCOP/EpiOcular/Cytosensor testing strategy could adequately classify antimicrobial cleaning products across all four EPA ocular hazard categories. Consequently, the strategy was not recommended for regulatory use at that time and additional product data and analyses were requested. Subsequent evidence expanded the demonstrated applicability of the component assays, particularly BCOP, and supported implementation of the integrated framework. EPA introduced the alternative testing policy in 2013 and expanded it in 2015, concluding that the strategy was acceptable for determining eye-hazard classification and labelling of antimicrobial cleaning products and could satisfy the corresponding in vivo eye-irritation data requirement. Status: resolved for antimicrobial cleaning products; application to other pesticide classes remains case-by-case.",
"TM1998-05": "[Access/implementation barrier] The original LLNA relies on radiolabelled thymidine or iodine to quantify lymphocyte proliferation. The need for licensed radioactive-material handling, specialised facilities and radioactive-waste disposal created a documented practical implementation barrier, and the independent peer-review panel explicitly identified development of a non-radioactive endpoint as a desirable future improvement. This limitation subsequently contributed to the development and regulatory adoption of non-radioactive LLNA variants and, more recently, a lymph-node cell-count read-out in the updated OECD TG 429. Status: largely mitigated by alternative non-radioactive read-outs; it did not prevent regulatory adoption of the LLNA itself.",
"TM2013-04": "[Applicability domain] The applicability domain of the ROS assay is restricted by physicochemical and assay-interference limitations. Poorly soluble substances may not be testable under the standard protocol, while reducing agents and other compounds that interfere with the singlet-oxygen or superoxide detection reactions can produce unreliable or uninterpretable results. These limitations were explicitly recognised during peer review and remain reflected in OECD TG 495. This represents a documented applicability-domain bottleneck affecting implementation for certain chemical classes. Status: active, partly mitigated by alternative solvents or modified procedures, but these require additional qualification and standardisation. [Predictive performance] The ROS assay was intentionally optimised as a highly sensitive screening method and showed no false-negative classifications for evaluable phototoxic reference chemicals in the validation studies. However, its non-biological test system can overpredict in vivo phototoxic potential because it does not account for tissue exposure, pharmacokinetics, photodegradation, or other biological determinants. Consequently, a positive ROS result is not sufficient as a stand-alone prediction of in vivo phototoxicity and generally requires follow-up testing within a tiered photosafety assessment strategy. This constitutes a documented predictive-performance limitation that affected how the method was positioned for regulatory use rather than preventing its eventual OECD adoption. Status: active, managed through integrated/tiered interpretation.",
"TM2016-10": "Historical validation bottlenecks — reproducibility, predictive performance and applicability domain: early validation phases showed insufficient inter-laboratory reproducibility and predictive performance, requiring iterative modification of the protocol and prediction model. Applicability limitations were also identified for specific chemical classes and assay-interfering substances. Subsequent validation demonstrated acceptable reproducibility and performance and the method was adopted in OECD TG 442E. Status: historical reproducibility and predictive-performance bottlenecks resolved for regulatory adoption; defined applicability limitations remain managed within the Test Guideline.",
"TM2011-15": "Predictive performance and applicability-domain limitations: the initial validation showed false-negative classifications and limited predictive performance for specific chemical types. Performance improved substantially after defining exclusions for acidic preparations and chemicals showing rapid phase separation. OECD TG 494 therefore applies an explicit applicability domain. The method also produces a relatively high proportion of false positives, which is acceptable within its intended bottom-up use because positive results require further assessment. Status: historical predictive-performance limitation mitigated through applicability-domain restrictions; defined applicability limitations remain.",
"TM2012-07": "Validation-readiness/evidence-package bottleneck: peer review of the initial validation package identified insufficient evidence to support final acceptance and requested a dedicated inter-laboratory study using the final protocol and a broader range of chemicals, including inactive substances and weak agonists and antagonists. A second inter-laboratory validation study addressed this recommendation and demonstrated high reproducibility and predictive performance. Status: resolved before regulatory adoption.",
"TM2008-17": "Historical validation-readiness and predictive-performance bottleneck: peer review identified concerns regarding the definition and justification of the positive-response criterion, particularly the PC10 endpoint, for which false-positive and equivocal responses were observed at low induction levels. Additional systematic inter-laboratory differences were noted, but overall reproducibility and protocol transferability were considered acceptable. Further validation was not considered essential for Test Guideline development. Status: resolved sufficiently for regulatory adoption; the method subsequently became a reference assay under OECD TG 455.",
"TM2016-12": "No documented bottleneck identified.",
"TM2008-14": "Historical validation-readiness, reproducibility and predictive-performance bottlenecks: OECD peer review raised concerns regarding within- and between-laboratory reproducibility, variability between tissue replicates, and particularly the false-negative classification of the strong irritant 1-bromohexane. Additional review and method refinement were required before inclusion in OECD TG 439. Modification of the washing procedure resolved the 1-bromohexane problem, and subsequent validation demonstrated acceptable reliability and predictive performance. Status: resolved before regulatory adoption.",
"TM2017-03": "Historical predictive-performance limitation: initial inter-laboratory evaluation showed that some UN GHS sub-categorisation performance measures did not meet the predefined LabCyte EPI-MODEL24 criteria, despite acceptable within- and between-laboratory reproducibility. In the expanded 79-chemical dataset, all predefined predictive-performance criteria were subsequently met, supporting inclusion of the method in OECD TG 431. Status: resolved in the evidence supporting regulatory adoption.",
"TM2015-04": "No documented bottleneck identified.",
"TM2019-05": "Validation-readiness and mechanistic-specificity bottleneck: during EURL ECVAM/EU-NETVAL assessment, the assay was considered insufficiently specific for detecting TPO inhibition. Consequently, the method definition and description could not be completed and further validation was stopped in March 2021. Status: unresolved; method discontinued.",
"TM2019-01": "Submission/evidence-readiness bottleneck: following PARERE consultation, EURL ECVAM invited the submitter to provide a revised presubmission rather than advancing the method. The public TSAR record does not specify the deficiencies requiring revision. No subsequent progression beyond presubmission assessment is documented. Status: unresolved in the TSAR lifecycle record.",
"TM2011-12": "No documented bottleneck identified. The original 2-TA submission was closed because an improved version was submitted as TM2012-05; this represents method evolution/supersession rather than a documented scientific or regulatory bottleneck.",
"TM2004-07": "Validation-readiness, reproducibility and standardisation bottlenecks: ESAC concluded that the improved BALB/c 3T3 CTA protocol was transferable, but within-laboratory reproducibility had not been sufficiently addressed and between-laboratory reproducibility, while promising, had not been fully demonstrated. Assay acceptance and assessment criteria, the statistical approach and data-interpretation procedure required further refinement, and additional dedicated testing was recommended before full validation or regulatory use. EURL ECVAM subsequently recommended continued use to expand the reproducibility database and confirm the statistical/data-interpretation procedure. The OECD Test Guideline project was later suspended in 2014. Status: unresolved for regulatory adoption.",
"TM2000-01": "No documented bottleneck identified.",
"TM2009-07": "The formal TSAR trajectory stopped during presubmission after additional information was requested but not provided. The exact zebrafish cardiotoxicity approach nevertheless progressed outside TSAR through development and publication of an automated high-throughput platform using 48-hpf zebrafish embryos to detect heart-rate changes, arrhythmia and cardiac arrest, including zERG-related effects relevant to QT prolongation. Subsequent performance testing with reference compounds and repeatability experiments provided substantial scientific progression beyond the lifecycle captured in TSAR. This supports continuing validation-readiness and programme-route bottlenecks. The published platform development does not by itself support unresolved reproducibility, predictive-performance or standardisation/adoption bottlenecks. No later formal EURL ECVAM validation, peer-review or OECD Test Guideline milestone for this cardiotoxicity method was identified.",
"TM1995-01": "Early prevalidation identified relatively low specificity and false-positive classifications, which prompted refinement of the method including incorporation of a dye-binding step. Subsequent validation demonstrated broad applicability, high inter-laboratory reproducibility and reliable discrimination between corrosive and non-corrosive chemicals. The method was subsequently scientifically endorsed and achieved international regulatory acceptance as OECD Test Guideline 430. The initially identified performance limitations therefore represent historical development issues that were addressed before regulatory adoption rather than unresolved bottlenecks of the final method.",
"TM2008-06": "The formal TSAR trajectory stopped at the presubmission stage after a full submission had been invited but no further communication was received. Independent validation evidence identified substantial method-performance limitations. CAMVA showed its strongest predictive performance for mild-to-moderate irritants and hydroalcoholic materials, while prediction was weaker at both the low and severe ends of the irritation spectrum. Biological variability, subjective vascular scoring and limitations of the available prediction models were identified as important contributors. The validation dataset also provided insufficient multi-laboratory evidence to establish robust transferability. Later studies demonstrated continued practical use within defined product domains, particularly personal-care formulations, but no convincing later formal validation, peer-review or OECD regulatory milestone for CAMVA itself was identified.",
"TM2010-04": "The formal TSAR trajectory stopped at the presubmission stage after EURL ECVAM requested a revised presubmission and no further communication was received. Development of the exact eye-irritation application nevertheless continued outside TSAR. The validated Cytosensor Microphysiometer INVITTOX No. 130 protocol was transferred to and automated on the IMOLA-IVD platform, followed by expansion of the dilution series and incorporation of impedance measurements as an additional morphological endpoint. Subsequent work aimed to improve prediction capability and develop a prediction model capable of addressing a broader range of eye-irritation classifications. These developments support continuing validation-readiness, predictive-performance, applicability and standardisation/adoption challenges. The work constitutes clear scientific progression beyond the lifecycle captured in TSAR, but no later formal validation, peer-review or OECD Test Guideline adoption of the IMOLA-IVD implementation itself was identified.",
"TM2009-10": "The formal TSAR trajectory stopped at the presubmission stage after EURL ECVAM invited a full submission, but no further communication or complete submission was received. This supports an evidence-readiness and programme-route bottleneck, but does not demonstrate failure of reproducibility, predictive performance or standardisation. Outside TSAR, the VitroDerm platform continued to be developed and applied, including derivative reconstructed-epidermis models for specific populations such as Baby VitroDerm and continued use for in vitro skin-irritation assessment. This constitutes scientific and implementation progression beyond the lifecycle captured in TSAR. However, no later formal EURL ECVAM validation, peer-review or OECD adoption milestone for the submitted THOR VitroDerm method was identified.",
"TM2011-02": "The formal TSAR trajectory stopped at the presubmission stage after EURL ECVAM invited a full submission but no further communication was received. Development nevertheless continued outside TSAR through inter-laboratory transfer, systematic Cosmetics Europe evaluation, testing of difficult substances and application to medical-device extracts. These studies support continued validation-readiness, reproducibility, predictive-performance and product/matrix-qualification challenges. Inter-laboratory results showed discrepancies between participating laboratories, external evaluations identified limitations in hazard and particularly potency prediction, and performance in medical-device extracts required further confirmation. Despite this substantial post-TSAR scientific progression, no formal validation, peer-review or OECD Test Guideline adoption was identified.",
"TM2012-05": "The formal TSAR trajectory stopped at the presubmission stage after a full submission had been invited but no further communication was received. Outside TSAR, development of the RhE IL-18 assay continued substantially, including refinement of prediction models, evaluation across different reconstructed human epidermis systems, further potency modelling and expansion to challenging chemical classes such as metal salts. These studies identified continuing evidence-readiness, predictive-performance, applicability and test-system qualification limitations: additional chemicals were required to establish robust prediction criteria, model-specific parameters required refinement, and several metal sensitizers were underestimated or not identified. Although RhE IL-18 subsequently appeared as an information source within OECD skin-sensitisation IATA guidance, no formal validation or OECD Test Guideline adoption was identified. The method therefore represents clear scientific progression beyond the lifecycle captured in TSAR, but without a later formal regulatory lifecycle milestone.",
"TM2008-13": "The TSAR trajectory stopped at the presubmission stage because no further communication was received from the submitter. This represents a programme-route discontinuation rather than evidence of failed validation or standardisation. Importantly, substantial method development occurred outside the TSAR lifecycle: a formal multi-laboratory validation study of EPISKIN, EpiDerm and SkinEthic reconstructed human epidermis models demonstrated feasibility for in vitro skin absorption testing. The validation identified relevant performance and applicability limitations: RHE models were generally more permeable than human epidermis and pig skin, and the positive validation conclusion was primarily supported for substances applied in aqueous solutions. Additional work was therefore required for more complex formulations and broader application domains. Despite this external validation, the RHE approach was not incorporated as the standard membrane system in OECD TG 428, which continues to describe excised human or animal skin.",
"TM2011-08": "The formal TSAR trajectory stopped at the submission stage because no further communication was received from the submitter after EURL ECVAM requested additional information. This supports an evidence-readiness and programme-route bottleneck, but does not by itself demonstrate technical failure or a standardisation barrier. Outside TSAR, the method continued to progress as the SensiDerm™ TMT-SRM assay: it was included in the Cosmetics Europe systematic evaluation of non-animal skin-sensitisation methods and was subsequently reported as pre-validated in two analytical laboratories using blinded and non-blinded test chemicals. The method therefore progressed substantially beyond the lifecycle captured in TSAR. However, no later formal EURL ECVAM validation, peer-review or OECD Test Guideline adoption was identified, and SensiDerm was not among the five methods subsequently selected for the Cosmetics Europe reference database.",
"TM2010-05": "Progression within TSAR stopped at the submission stage. Following presubmission assessment, the submitter was invited to provide a full submission in 2011, but no further submission was received and TSAR closed the process in 2020. Existing studies supported transferability and inter-laboratory reproducibility, while further evidence was still needed to establish predictive performance and regulatory integration for broader use. Regulators considered the assay potentially valuable as a component of an integrated testing strategy for reproductive toxicity. Importantly, the end of the TSAR trajectory did not represent the end of method development: the bovine oocyte maturation approach was subsequently taken up and further developed within the FREIA/EURION programme, including expanded endpoints for female reproductive endocrine-disruption testing. This constitutes post-TSAR scientific progression, but no later formal validation, peer-review or regulatory-adoption stage was identified.",
"TM2008-02": "Validation and peer-review progression initially encountered evidence-package, predictive-performance and applicability limitations. EURL ECVAM requested retesting of diethyl phthalate, additional statistical analysis and corrections to the submission, while peer review identified shortcomings in prospective study planning, blinding and training documentation. Three of twenty reference chemicals were consistently misclassified with the MTT endpoint, although subsequent analysis concluded that the assay met the required accuracy and reliability criteria. The MTT endpoint showed good within- and between-laboratory reproducibility; the secondary IL-1α endpoint was substantially more variable and was not retained as a useful classification endpoint. Applicability remained insufficiently established for several classes including volatiles, emulsions, mixtures, hydrolysing or polymerising chemicals, acids and bases. These historical issues did not prevent regulatory adoption: SkinEthic RHE was incorporated into OECD TG 439 in 2010 and remains included in the current guideline.",
"TM2008-01": "Historical predictive-performance and applicability-domain limitations were documented. The original EpiDerm skin irritation protocol had insufficient sensitivity, prompting modification of the exposure protocol and a follow-up validation study. During the 2008 peer review, additional statistical analysis of predictive performance was requested; however, transferability, within- and between-laboratory reproducibility, and overall accuracy and reliability subsequently met the applicable performance criteria. Applicability limitations included rapidly polymerising or hydrolysing chemicals, gases/vapours/aerosols, and potential increased variability for rapidly evaporating or plastic-reactive substances. These limitations did not prevent regulatory adoption: Modified EpiDerm SIT was incorporated into OECD TG 439 in 2010 and remains a validated reference method in the 2025 guideline.",
"TM2006-02": "Validation and regulatory progression of Ocular Irritection was initially constrained by evidence-readiness, predictive-performance, mechanistic, matrix-qualification, applicability and standardisation issues. EURL ECVAM requested repeated revisions of the submission, and the 2016 ESAC peer review considered the mechanistic relevance poorly defined, identified insufficient characterisation and batch-consistency evidence for the proprietary macromolecular matrix, documented applicability and predictive-performance limitations, and found the proposed Performance Standards incomplete. ESAC therefore did not recommend the assay for regulatory testing at that time. These barriers were subsequently addressed sufficiently for OECD adoption: supplemental matrix-characterisation evidence was assessed by the OECD expert group and Ocular Irritection became the validated reference method under OECD TG 496. Residual applicability limitations remain, and the assay is not a stand-alone replacement across the full range of eye irritation/serious eye damage.",
"TM2005-04": "The EpiSkin Skin Irritation Test successfully completed prospective validation, demonstrated adequate predictive performance and reproducibility, and was adopted under OECD TG 439 as a replacement for in vivo rabbit skin irritation testing. The principal current implementation barrier is commercial availability: the EpiSkin model has no longer been commercially available since December 2024 and was consequently removed from the current OECD TG 439 for prospective data generation. Historical data generated with EpiSkin remain covered by OECD Mutual Acceptance of Data. Applicability limitations inherent to TG 439 remain for certain test materials, including gases, aerosols and some difficult-to-test chemicals or mixtures.",
"TM2009-13": "CryoHeps successfully completed EURL ECVAM validation and independent ESAC peer review and was initially included in OECD Test Guideline development for CYP induction. However, cryopreserved primary human hepatocytes showed substantial donor- and batch-dependent variability, resulting in lower reproducibility than the parallel HepaRG test system. For regulatory chemical screening, the OECD Expert Group prioritised a more reproducible, readily available and standardisable test system and therefore continued Test Guideline development with the HepaRG method while stopping the CryoHeps route. The principal bottlenecks therefore concern reproducibility/transferability, practical implementation and availability, programme/route selection and standardisation rather than mechanistic relevance or failure of validation.",
"TM2007-01": "No documented bottleneck identified.",
"TM1999-05": "No documented bottleneck identified. The validation study reported some inter-laboratory variability and laboratory-specific experimental issues, but these did not prevent successful validation, positive ESAC peer review, or subsequent regulatory adoption in the European Pharmacopoeia.",
"TM1999-04": "No documented bottleneck identified. The validation study reported some inter-laboratory variability and laboratory-specific experimental issues, but these did not prevent successful validation, positive ESAC peer review, or subsequent regulatory adoption in the European Pharmacopoeia.",
"TM2002-02": "Applicability domain and product/matrix qualification: ESAC concluded that Cryo-WBT-IL1 was scientifically validated for the detection and quantification of pyrogenicity mediated by Gram-negative endotoxins, but considered the available evidence insufficient at that time to confirm full validation across the broader non-endotoxin pyrogen domain. Regulatory use was also subject to product-specific validation. Subsequent European Pharmacopoeia development substantially broadened and strengthened the regulatory role of the MAT, culminating in the removal of the rabbit pyrogen test from the Ph. Eur.; however, suitability and interference testing for the specific product or matrix remain required. The historical applicability-domain limitation has therefore largely been superseded, whereas product/matrix qualification remains an implementation requirement.",
"TM2002-05": "Applicability domain and product/matrix qualification: ESAC concluded that WBT-IL6 was scientifically validated for the detection and quantification of pyrogenicity mediated by Gram-negative endotoxins, but considered the available evidence insufficient at that time to confirm full validation across the broader non-endotoxin pyrogen domain. Regulatory use was also subject to product-specific validation. Subsequent European Pharmacopoeia development substantially broadened and strengthened the regulatory role of the MAT; however, suitability and interference testing for the specific product or matrix remain required. The historical applicability-domain limitation has therefore largely been superseded, whereas product/matrix qualification remains an implementation requirement.",
"TM2002-03": "Applicability domain and product/matrix qualification: ESAC concluded that MM6 IL6 was scientifically validated for the detection and quantification of pyrogenicity mediated by Gram-negative endotoxins, but considered the available evidence insufficient at that time to confirm full validation across the broader non-endotoxin pyrogen domain. Regulatory use was also subject to product-specific validation. Subsequent European Pharmacopoeia development substantially broadened and strengthened the regulatory role of the MAT; however, suitability and interference testing for the specific product or matrix remain required. The historical applicability-domain limitation has therefore largely been superseded, whereas product/matrix qualification remains an implementation requirement.",
"TM2002-01": "Applicability domain and product/matrix qualification: ESAC concluded that WBT-IL1 was scientifically validated for the detection and quantification of pyrogenicity mediated by Gram-negative endotoxins, but considered the available evidence insufficient at that time to confirm full validation across the broader non-endotoxin pyrogen domain. Regulatory use was also subject to product-specific validation. Subsequent European Pharmacopoeia development substantially broadened and strengthened the regulatory role of the MAT; however, suitability and interference testing for the specific product or matrix remain required. The historical applicability-domain limitation has therefore largely been superseded, whereas product/matrix qualification remains an implementation requirement.",
"TM2002-04": "Applicability domain and product/matrix qualification: ESAC concluded that PBMC-IL6 was scientifically validated for the detection and quantification of pyrogenicity mediated by Gram-negative endotoxins, but considered the available evidence insufficient at that time to confirm full validation across the broader non-endotoxin pyrogen domain. Regulatory use was also subject to product-specific validation. Subsequent European Pharmacopoeia development substantially broadened and strengthened the regulatory role of the MAT; however, suitability and interference testing for the specific product or matrix remain required. The historical applicability-domain limitation has therefore largely been superseded, whereas product/matrix qualification remains an implementation requirement.",
"TM2005-05": "No documented bottleneck identified. The threshold approach was positively endorsed by ESAC as a valid strategy to substantially reduce fish use and was subsequently adopted in OECD Guidance Document 126. Progression to a full OECD TG 203 study when mortality occurs at the threshold concentration is an intended decision rule of the tiered strategy rather than a lifecycle bottleneck.",
"TM1998-03": "No documented bottleneck identified. EpiDerm™ successfully progressed through ECVAM validation and peer review to regulatory adoption in OECD TG 431. Subsequent protocol refinements addressing assay interference and corrosivity sub-categorisation represent post-adoption method maintenance rather than a lifecycle bottleneck. EpiDerm™ remains an endorsed validated reference method in the 2025 revision of OECD TG 431.",
"TM1998-02": "Access/implementation barrier: EpiSkin™ successfully progressed through validation, positive ESAC peer review and regulatory adoption in OECD TG 431. However, OECD removed the EpiSkin™ model from the 2025 revision of TG 431 because it is no longer commercially available. Supporting EpiSkin™ references and data remain in the guideline for data evaluation and Performance Standards, but the exact model is no longer available for new data generation. This represents a current post-adoption access/implementation barrier rather than a validation or regulatory-acceptance failure.",
"TM1998-01": "Applicability domain and test-chemical qualification: the original ECVAM validation did not support Corrositex® across the full range of tested chemicals, and ESAC concluded that the method was scientifically validated only for acids, bases and their derivatives that meet the technical requirements of the assay. This limitation remains explicit in OECD TG 435: many non-corrosive and some corrosive chemicals may fail the initial compatibility test, and chemicals that cannot be detected by the chemical detection system cannot be evaluated with the method. These applicability and chemical-qualification limitations did not prevent regulatory adoption, but they restrict the domain in which the method can be used. No separate validation-readiness bottleneck is retained because the evidence concerns method scope and compatibility rather than an incomplete validation package.",
"TM2008-10": "[Validation/readiness] Formal validation and independent scientific review were required before regulatory uptake. This barrier was resolved through validation/peer review and OECD guideline adoption. Status: resolved. [Product/matrix qualification] Early evidence for representative labelled products and mixtures across sectoral use classes was limited; supplementary testing expanded confidence in product-level applicability. Status: largely resolved/managed. [Regulatory relevance/integration] EpiOcular EIT can identify chemicals not requiring classification, but it is not a stand-alone method for discrimination across all UN GHS eye-hazard categories. Classified responses require complementary testing or integration in a defined/testing strategy. Status: active but formally managed.",
"TM2015-03": "[Validation/performance assessment] Initial implementation required demonstration of reliability, reproducibility and predictive performance of the reconstructed human corneal epithelium model across relevant chemicals. Validation and inter-laboratory evidence supported acceptance. Status: resolved/managed. [Regulatory relevance/integration] SkinEthic HCE EIT is useful for identifying chemicals not requiring classification but is not a stand-alone method for resolving all UN GHS eye-hazard categories; classified responses require complementary information or integrated approaches. Status: active but managed. [Standardisation/regulatory adoption] Harmonised protocols, tissue performance criteria and consistent implementation were required for regulatory confidence; OECD standardisation substantially reduced this barrier. Status: largely resolved/managed.",
"TM1997-01": "[Evidence package/justification] Removal of an established animal-based batch-safety requirement required sufficient evidence and regulatory experience to demonstrate that vaccine quality and safety could be maintained without the additional animal test. Status: resolved. [Regulatory relevance/integration] The principal transition challenge was regulatory implementation: authorities had to accept removal/waiver of the Target Animal Batch Safety Test while preserving the quality-control framework. This was resolved through regulatory implementation and deletion of the requirement in Europe. Status: resolved.",
"TM2005-01": "[Validation/performance assessment] Regulatory uptake required demonstration that the in vitro mammalian cell micronucleus assay reliably detects chromosomal damage with adequate predictive performance. Validation evidence supported confidence in performance. Status: resolved through validation and guideline adoption. [Regulatory relevance/integration] MNvit is an important component of genotoxicity assessment but is not a stand-alone replacement for all genetic-toxicity questions; results are interpreted with complementary assays and weight-of-evidence. Status: active but managed. [Standardisation/regulatory adoption] Differences in cell systems, protocols and scoring approaches required harmonisation for consistent implementation. OECD TG 487 and standardised performance criteria substantially reduced this barrier. Status: largely resolved/managed.",
"TM2001-01": "[Reproducibility/transferability] In the 2007 Suvaxyn Ery referral, variability between batches of mice in the in-vivo component produced large variation in serological reference-batch results and reduced the assay's ability to demonstrate reference-batch equivalence. CVMP recommended a more robust potency test, for example using reference serum instead of a reference vaccine. Adoption of the method was unaffected; implementation of the recommended change has not been verified. Status: historical, resolution unverified.",
"TM2015-02": "[Validation/readiness] EURL ECVAM identified unspecified shortcomings in the May 2015 presubmission that required attention before the proposed Performance Standards-based validation study; the submission did not progress. No correction or completed validation for this exact TM is documented. Status: historical, resolution unverified. [Programme/route selection] EURL ECVAM did not pursue follow-up of this similar RhE method and advised the developers to explore an OECD National Coordinator route for a potential direct submission after successful validation. Whether that route was undertaken is unverified. Status: historical.",
"TM2004-04": "[Validation/readiness] The ECVAM/ESAC peer review identified significant shortcomings and inconsistencies in specificity and sensitivity, protocol deviations, prediction-model problems, variable data quality and insufficient evidence for required validation modules. [Reproducibility/transferability] Within-laboratory variability was difficult to assess, with CVs up to 67%; no evidence for transferability was available and reported inter-laboratory CVs were considered unacceptably high. [Predictive performance] Predictive capacity against standard classification systems was poor, with substantial Top-Down over-classification and Bottom-Up misclassification. [Applicability domain] Evaluation was primarily based on water-soluble test materials and several chemical/material classes fell outside the supported domain. [Regulatory relevance/integration; Standardisation/adoption] Performance standards were unavailable and the method was not considered ready for regulatory use as either a Top-Down or Bottom-Up screen, including within a tiered-testing strategy. OECD guidance in 2024 still reports that further work is required before scientific validity can be established. Status: unresolved/current.",
"TM2004-03": "[Validation/readiness/evidence package] During ECVAM/ESAC review, the evidence package for NRR was insufficiently mature for general regulatory recommendation: Protocol 54 had a limited and uneven dataset, only one original high-quality dataset, and required further protocol refinement and performance criteria. [Reproducibility/transferability] Within-laboratory variability was high (mean CV 22–85% for Protocol 54), while transferability and between-laboratory evidence were insufficient or unavailable. [Predictive performance] NRR showed high sensitivity but lower specificity and substantial overprediction/false positives for some material classes. [Applicability domain] High-quality evidence supported only a restricted domain, particularly hydro-alcoholic and surfactant-based formulations, with insufficient or problematic performance for several other material types. [Regulatory relevance/integration] ESAC considered NRR potentially useful as a screen or component of a tiered strategy, but not adequately supported as a stand-alone replacement for general regulatory eye-irritation assessment. [Standardisation/adoption] Performance standards were lacking and ESAC did not recommend general regulatory consideration. These barriers remain historically relevant despite limited national regulatory use and later inclusion of NRR evidence in regulatory assessment frameworks.",
"TM2009-05": "[Validation/readiness/evidence package] During the EURL ECVAM-coordinated validation study, the original MUSST prediction model allowed three final outcomes (Sensitiser, Non-sensitiser and Inconclusive). The unresolved handling of inconclusive outcomes meant that the method was not sufficiently mature to continue in its submitted form. The original validation route was stopped and the prediction model/protocol was subsequently revised under U-SENS™. Status: resolved through method revision and resubmission. [Reproducibility/transferability] MUSST was withdrawn after the first phase of the EURL ECVAM validation study because inconclusive classifications generated by the original prediction model adversely affected reproducibility across laboratories. The prediction model was subsequently optimised and the revised U-SENS™ method underwent new transferability and reproducibility studies. Status: historical bottleneck, addressed in the successor method.",
"TM2009-11": "[Reproducibility/transferability] During early prevalidation, initial transferability problems were encountered and were resolved by isolating a new ER\\u03b1-CALUX cell clone with improved stability and luciferase response. Subsequent formal OECD-oriented validation demonstrated high within- and between-laboratory reproducibility. Status: historical bottleneck, resolved before regulatory adoption. [Applicability domain] OECD TG 455 identifies limitations of ER transactivation assays including limited metabolic capacity of the in vitro cell system, inability to directly extrapolate results to the intact endocrine system, and lack of validation for mixtures. Status: current scope limitation despite regulatory adoption. [Regulatory relevance/integration] ER\\u03b1-CALUX provides Level-2 mechanistic information and is intended primarily for screening, prioritisation and use within weight-of-evidence approaches rather than as a stand-alone demonstration of endocrine disruption in vivo. Status: intrinsic regulatory-integration limitation. [Programme prioritisation/route selection] EURL ECVAM determined that ER\\u03b1-CALUX qualified for performance-standards-based validation but did not prioritise it for an EURL ECVAM-coordinated validation study. Validation therefore proceeded externally and the method subsequently entered the OECD Test Guidelines route. Status: historical route-selection bottleneck, resolved through external validation and OECD adoption.",
"TM2004-01": "[Predictive performance] The Cytosensor Microphysiometer showed sufficient performance for defined classification decisions but could not reliably identify moderate and mild ocular irritants, preventing use across the full eye-irritation classification range. False-positive limitations were also reported for some bottom-up applications. Status: persistent limitation of the validated decision domain. [Applicability domain] Validated regulatory use was restricted to defined chemical domains: water-soluble chemicals for identification of severe/corrosive eye irritants and water-soluble surfactants or surfactant-containing mixtures for identification of non-classified materials. Status: intrinsic scope limitation. [Regulatory relevance/integration] The method was supported as an initial component of Top-Down and Bottom-Up testing strategies rather than as a stand-alone method covering all eye-irritation categories. Status: complementary-evidence requirement. [Access/implementation] OECD Test Guideline development was stopped because the validated Cytosensor technology was no longer commercially available. Status: decisive implementation bottleneck. [Standardisation/regulatory adoption] An OECD Test Guideline project was initiated and a draft guideline developed, but formal international standardisation was stopped in 2016 because the validated technology was unavailable. Status: OECD adoption not completed; regulatory implementation nevertheless occurred through specific US pathways.",
"TM2009-12": "[Validation/readiness/evidence package] At the time of development, the neuronal AChE/NTE approach had promising concordance with available in vivo organophosphorus neurotoxicity data, but the evidence package had not progressed to a formal inter-laboratory validation study and the classification approach remained primarily a screening/prediction framework. Status: evidence-readiness limitation remained unresolved when the EURL ECVAM submission route stopped. [Applicability domain] The method is specifically applicable to organophosphorus/anticholinesterase neurotoxicity and depends on AChE/NTE-mediated mechanisms. Compounds requiring metabolic bioactivation may require an additional exogenous activation system, and delayed or chronic neurotoxicity operating through mechanisms outside the AChE/NTE paradigm may not be detected. Status: intrinsic scope and biotransformation limitation. [Regulatory relevance/integration] The method was developed primarily as a first screening/ranking component and part of a test battery rather than as a complete stand-alone replacement for the existing delayed-neurotoxicity assessment. Status: complementary-evidence requirement. [Programme prioritisation/route selection] Following full-submission assessment and consultation with EFSA, PARERE and ESTAF, EURL ECVAM informed the submitter in February 2012 that it did not intend to pursue the submission further. The public TSAR record does not identify a specific technical failure as the reason, so no additional causal bottleneck is inferred from the stopping decision itself.",
"TM2010-06": "[Validation/readiness/evidence package] The embryonic rat DRG organotypic-culture assay showed promising discrimination of neurotoxic and non-neurotoxic antineoplastic reference compounds and encouraging inter-observer/inter-laboratory consistency, but the supporting evidence remained limited in chemical number and scope and the method had not progressed to formal validation. Status: evidence-readiness limitation remained unresolved when the EURL ECVAM submission route stopped. [Applicability domain] The submitted evidence primarily supported detection of peripheral neurotoxicity from antineoplastic drugs through effects on NGF-induced neurite outgrowth in embryonic rat sensory ganglia. This represents a narrower domain than neurotoxicity generally and may not capture neurotoxic mechanisms that do not substantially affect this endpoint. Status: intrinsic scope limitation. [Regulatory relevance/integration] The method was proposed mainly for early screening and discovery and relies principally on a morphometric neurite-outgrowth endpoint; the available evidence did not establish it as a complete stand-alone regulatory neurotoxicity assessment. Status: complementary-evidence/integration requirement. [Programme prioritisation/route selection] Following assessment of the full submission, EURL ECVAM informed the submitter in July 2012 that it did not intend to pursue the submission further. The public TSAR record does not identify a specific technical failure as the cause, so no additional causal bottleneck is inferred from the stopping decision.",
"TM2014-04": "[Validation/readiness/evidence package] devTOXqP accumulated substantial predictive and use-case evidence after the original TSAR submission, but formal regulatory qualification has required additional analytical validation, independent transfer and interlaboratory reproducibility evidence. These activities remain part of the ongoing FDA Biomarker Qualification pathway. Status: being actively addressed through external qualification. [Reproducibility/transferability] Regulatory qualification required transfer of the assay from the originating laboratory to an independent laboratory and generation of blinded interlaboratory reproducibility data. Recent studies indicate successful transfer, but this requirement represented a material qualification step. Status: historical/qualification bottleneck being resolved. [Predictive performance] Large-scale ToxCast evaluation demonstrated useful overall accuracy and high specificity but more modest sensitivity against broader in-vivo developmental-toxicity reference data, indicating that predictive performance depends on the reference set and intended context of use. Status: context-dependent limitation. [Applicability domain] The undifferentiated human pluripotent-cell model primarily reflects early developmental biology; later developmental processes, ADME and maternal-fetal interactions are not represented directly and exposure interpretation may require IVIVE/PBK modelling. Status: intrinsic scope limitation. [Mechanistic relevance] The ornithine/cystine metabolic biomarker is associated with developmental toxicity but does not represent the full range of biological processes involved in embryo-fetal development. FDA therefore narrowed the proposed qualification context rather than treating the assay as a complete Annex-2 alternative assay. Status: mechanistic-coverage limitation. [Regulatory relevance/integration] EURL ECVAM stopped follow-up after PARERE consultation on regulatory relevance. FDA subsequently accepted the biomarker into its qualification programme with a narrowed context of use as part of a weight-of-evidence assessment. Status: regulatory integration remains context-specific. [Programme prioritisation/route selection] The EURL ECVAM route stopped after PARERE consultation, while further development proceeded through EPA research and FDA biomarker-qualification pathways. Status: alternative external route established.",
"TM2012-02": "[Validation/readiness/evidence package] The Skin PAMPA submission was placed on hold pending broader EURL ECVAM work on toxicokinetics/ADME and was subsequently stopped before formal validation. Later studies continued to optimise assay conditions and expand the supporting evidence base. Status: regulatory-validation readiness was not completed within the TSAR route. [Predictive performance] Skin PAMPA shows useful correlations with human and animal skin permeability for several chemical and formulation datasets, but performance is not universal; independent comparisons have reported substantial quantitative differences or overprediction relative to human skin. Status: context-dependent predictive limitation. [Applicability domain] The synthetic membrane primarily models passive diffusion through the stratum-corneum lipid barrier and does not reproduce the full biological complexity of skin, including metabolism, cellular components and other physiological processes. Status: intrinsic scope limitation. [Product/matrix qualification] Membrane behaviour and permeability can be affected by formulation components, solvents, penetration enhancers, receptor-medium additives and incompatible excipients; later studies specifically investigated these compatibility limits. Status: formulation-dependent qualification requirement. [Regulatory relevance/integration] Skin PAMPA has been implemented primarily as a high-throughput screening and formulation-ranking tool, whereas established regulatory dermal-absorption approaches such as OECD TG 428 continue to use excised biological skin. Status: complementary/screening role rather than stand-alone regulatory replacement. [Programme prioritisation/route selection] EURL ECVAM placed the submission on hold pending completion of wider toxicokinetics work and subsequently stopped the submission, while indicating that follow-up might be considered within a broader ADME context. Status: TSAR route stopped; method development and practical implementation continued externally.",
"TM2013-03": "[Validation/readiness/evidence package] The Skin Sensitizer Predictor demonstrated promising initial hazard-prediction performance, including blinded classification, but the supporting dataset remained small and no independent multi-laboratory validation of the complete assay and prediction model was identified. The EURL ECVAM route stopped after presubmission assessment. Status: regulatory-validation readiness remained incomplete. [Regulatory relevance/integration] The assay measures dendritic-cell gene-expression and intracellular-signalling responses and integrates these readouts through a statistical classifier. The original developers described the approach as a model to be integrated into a future in vitro testing platform rather than establishing it as a complete stand-alone regulatory skin-sensitisation assessment. This supports use as a component of an integrated test battery or weight-of-evidence approach. Current OECD defined approaches likewise combine information across multiple skin-sensitisation key events. No evidence was identified that the exact Skin Sensitizer Predictor was formally adopted as a component of an OECD Defined Approach. Status: complementary/integration requirement. [Programme prioritisation/route selection] Following assessment of the presubmission, EURL ECVAM informed the submitter in February 2014 that the method was not considered for follow-up. The public TSAR record does not provide a more specific technical cause. Status: TSAR route stopped; proprietary development and practical deployment continued outside the TSAR process.",
"TM2008-03": "[Validation/readiness/evidence package] PorCORA showed encouraging correlation with historical Draize data and subsequently underwent exact-method validation-oriented evaluation outside TSAR, including a 2011 study of 32 reference compounds and later internal validation on a 56-chemical dataset. However, the original EURL ECVAM submission was not selected for coordinated validation, and no independent formal multi-laboratory validation programme was identified. Status: substantial evidence development occurred externally, but formal regulatory-validation readiness remained incomplete. [Predictive performance] Exact-method studies reported generally high performance, including 28/32 correct predictions and approximately 88-91% accuracy for several regulatory hazard classification schemes, but documented misclassifications and context-dependent product results show that predictive performance was not universal. Status: generally strong but non-universal predictive performance. [Regulatory relevance/integration] PorCORA specifically provides information on corneal-injury reversibility, an endpoint not supplied by several established alternative ocular assays. Both the original method description and later industrial application position PorCORA as complementary to other methods such as BCOP and as a component of a weight-of-evidence assessment rather than a complete stand-alone ocular hazard test. Status: integration with complementary evidence remains required. [Programme prioritisation/route selection] Following full-submission assessment, EURL ECVAM informed the submitter in March 2009 that PorCORA would not be considered for EURL ECVAM-coordinated validation. The public TSAR record does not identify a more specific technical cause. Status: TSAR route stopped; exact-method validation-oriented development and later practical implementation continued externally.",
"TM2014-07": "[Validation/readiness/evidence package] CORNiPSC entered EURL ECVAM only at presubmission stage and the JRC assessment indicated that further refinement of the eye-irritation protocol was required. Subsequent related-platform development improved production of iPSC-derived ocular epithelial cells and demonstrated cytotoxicity responses to model irritants, but no broad regulatory reference-chemical dataset or formal independent validation programme was identified. Status: validation readiness remained incomplete. [Applicability domain] The JRC assessment indicated that the cell-culture format was expected to be applicable primarily to water-soluble chemicals, creating a concrete chemical-domain restriction. Status: intrinsic applicability limitation. [Regulatory relevance/integration] CORNiPSC was explicitly proposed as a potential initial component of a Bottom-Up or Top-Down integrated testing strategy rather than as a complete stand-alone eye-irritation assessment. Status: complementary-evidence/integration requirement. [Programme prioritisation/route selection] EURL ECVAM stopped the presubmission in May 2015 and informed the submitter that the method was not considered for follow-up; no more specific technical cause is stated in the public TSAR record. Status: TSAR route stopped while related iPSC ocular-epithelial method development subsequently continued outside TSAR. [Access/implementation barrier] Later development of the iPSC-derived ocular epithelial platform identified consistent production of starting iPSCs and differentiated cells at the quantity, quality and purity required by pharmaceutical users as a major challenge for commercialisation. Status: implementation/scalability barrier.",
"TM2004-02": "[Validation/readiness/evidence package] During retrospective validation and peer review, the FL evidence package contained limitations in data robustness, protocol definition and performance standards, and additional refinement and testing were considered necessary before regulatory use. Subsequent regulatory development narrowed the intended purpose and standardised the assay in OECD TG 460. Status: historical bottleneck, resolved/managed for the defined regulatory use. [Reproducibility/transferability] The original INVITTOX 71 evidence showed substantial within-laboratory and operator variability, poor protocol transfer and low interlaboratory agreement. The current OECD guideline specifies the test procedure, controls and proficiency chemicals for laboratories implementing the assay. Status: historical bottleneck, largely resolved/managed through standardisation and proficiency requirements. [Predictive performance] Early validation showed incomplete concordance across broader eye-irritation categories and insufficient evidence for some materials and formulations. Regulatory use was subsequently restricted to identification of ocular corrosives/severe irritants within the validated applicability domain rather than prediction across the full irritation spectrum. Status: historical limitation managed by narrowing the context of use. [Applicability domain] OECD TG 460 remains restricted primarily to water-soluble chemicals; strong acids and bases, fixatives and highly volatile substances are excluded, while coloured, viscous and membrane-binding chemicals can present additional limitations. Status: active applicability-domain limitation. [Regulatory relevance/integration] FL is regulatorily accepted for identifying Category 1 ocular hazards within its defined domain but is not a complete stand-alone replacement across all eye-irritation categories and is intended for use within a tiered/IATA approach. Status: active integration requirement.",
"TM1999-01": "[Validation/readiness/evidence package] EST was formally validated and endorsed by ESAC, but subsequent follow-up evaluations showed that the original evidence base did not support sufficiently robust stand-alone regulatory use across a broader chemical domain. Status: historical validation success with unresolved readiness limitations for broad regulatory application. [Predictive performance] The original ECVAM validation reported approximately 78% overall accuracy and excellent performance for strongly embryotoxic chemicals, but later follow-up testing with additional chemicals showed substantially poorer classification performance. Status: predictive performance was not sufficiently robust across expanded chemical sets. [Applicability domain] The classical EST models only selected developmental processes and a limited developmental window, and performance varies across chemical classes and mechanisms. Status: active applicability-domain limitation. [Mechanistic relevance] The classical assay relies heavily on inhibition of differentiation of mouse embryonic stem cells into beating cardiomyocytes together with cytotoxicity endpoints; this captures only part of developmental biology and lacks maternal metabolism and broader organogenesis. Status: active mechanistic-coverage limitation. [Regulatory relevance/integration] Although scientifically validated, EST was not established as a stand-alone replacement for regulatory reproductive/developmental toxicity assessment and is better suited as a component of a broader testing strategy. Status: active integration requirement. [Access/implementation barrier] The classical protocol is relatively lengthy and labour-intensive, with manual assessment of cardiomyocyte differentiation and limited throughput; later development therefore pursued more objective and automatable readouts. Status: implementation/throughput limitation.",
"TM1999-02": "[Validation/readiness/evidence package] The formal ECVAM study validated the rat limb-bud micromass (MM) assay, but later regulatory-application review concluded that the evidence base was insufficient for broad stand-alone regulatory use: the validation set was limited, important chemical classes and mechanisms were underrepresented, and translation of effective in vitro concentrations to relevant in vivo exposure remained unresolved. Status: active evidence-readiness limitation for broad regulatory application. [Reproducibility/transferability] The broader micromass approach originally included rat embryonic midbrain cultures, but those cultures could not be established reproducibly even in experienced laboratories; formal validation therefore focused on the rat limb-bud protocol, which subsequently showed acceptable intra- and inter-laboratory reproducibility. Status: historical limitation, managed by narrowing and standardising the test system. [Predictive performance] The validated limb-bud MM showed approximately 70% overall accuracy, with weaker performance for non-embryotoxic and weakly embryotoxic chemicals than for strongly embryotoxic chemicals. Status: active predictive-performance limitation outside the strong-embryotoxicant use case. [Applicability domain] The validation set contained only 20 chemicals and did not adequately cover important chemical classes or mechanisms; the assay primarily models limb-bud chondrogenesis rather than the full developmental-toxicity domain. Status: active applicability limitation. [Mechanistic relevance] MM captures disruption of growth and differentiation in embryonic limb-bud mesenchyme but lacks maternal physiology/metabolism and broader organogenesis. Status: active mechanistic-coverage limitation. [Regulatory relevance/integration] EURL ECVAM evidence positions MM as supportive/testing-strategy evidence for identifying strong embryotoxicants rather than a stand-alone regulatory replacement for developmental-toxicity assessment. Status: active integration requirement. [Access/implementation barrier] The validated method requires freshly isolated embryonic limb-bud cells obtained from pregnant rats, creating animal-supply and primary-cell preparation requirements that constrain practical implementation relative to permanent-cell-line NAMs. Status: active implementation limitation.",
"TM1999-03": "[Validation/readiness/evidence package] WEC was formally validated and endorsed by ESAC, but subsequent practical-regulatory review identified additional requirements before broad stand-alone regulatory use. Status: active readiness limitation for broad regulatory application. [Reproducibility/transferability] During method development, the original elaborate morphological scoring system could not be applied reproducibly enough across laboratories, leading to selection/refinement of endpoints and prediction models. The final validated WEC protocol subsequently showed acceptable reproducibility. Status: historical limitation, managed during validation. [Predictive performance] The WEC-only prediction model (PM1) showed lower overall performance, whereas the better performing PM2 incorporated additional 3T3 cytotoxicity information from outside the WEC assay itself. Status: active predictive-performance limitation for WEC as a stand-alone predictor. [Applicability domain] WEC models a defined postimplantation developmental window and its interpretation is affected by chemical-domain and in-vitro-to-in-vivo extrapolation constraints. Status: active applicability-domain limitation. [Mechanistic relevance] WEC captures substantial organogenesis in an intact embryo but lacks the maternal organism, placental context and full maternal metabolism/exposure kinetics. Status: active mechanistic-context limitation. [Regulatory relevance/integration] The strongest validated prediction model combines WEC with additional cytotoxicity information, and later ECVAM/JRC evidence positions WEC mainly within tiered or integrated developmental-toxicity strategies rather than as a complete stand-alone regulatory replacement. Status: active integration requirement. [Access/implementation barrier] WEC is technically demanding, relatively low-throughput and requires fresh intact rat embryos obtained from pregnant animals. Status: active implementation and biological-material barrier.",
"TM2009-02": "[Validation/readiness/evidence package] EURL ECVAM validation progressed through transferability and reproducibility work but was stopped before completion of the planned predictive-capacity/prediction-model assessment, leaving the validation package incomplete for broad regulatory use. Status: active/unresolved validation-readiness limitation. [Applicability domain] Follow-up validation planning explicitly required definition of the prediction model and application domain before the assay could be fully characterised for regulatory use. Status: unresolved applicability-domain limitation at the end of the EURL ECVAM validation route. [Regulatory relevance/integration] MELN measures a mechanistic estrogen-receptor transactivation event and is suitable as screening/weight-of-evidence evidence within endocrine-disruption IATA rather than as a complete stand-alone hazard decision. Status: active integration requirement. [Programme prioritisation/route selection] EURL ECVAM stopped the validation programme after consultation with the submitter. Status: documented validation-route stop. [Access/implementation barrier] The TSAR validation route was stopped because agreement could not be reached on conditions for material transfer, creating a concrete access/implementation barrier for continued validation. Status: documented access barrier. [Standardisation/regulatory adoption] The exact MELN assay subsequently entered OECD Test Guidelines Programme Project 4.64 for intended integration into TG 455, but that standardisation project was later suspended because of cell-contamination problems. Status: documented standardisation/adoption barrier after TSAR.",
"TM2009-09": "[Validation readiness / evidence package] In May 2012, EURL ECVAM replied that within- and between-laboratory variability in the initial submission did not meet performance standards criteria; resolved through protocol optimisation and supplementary validation data. Status: historical / resolved. [Predictive performance] In December 2014, ESAC requested supplementary testing at two laboratories to eliminate potential bias in the chemical testing dataset; successfully addressed in 2015 leading to ESAC Opinion 2016-05. Status: historical / resolved. [Standardisation / regulatory adoption] Fully resolved: officially adopted under OECD Test Guideline 439 in June 2019.",
"TM2013-02": "No documented bottleneck identified; successfully validated, recommended by EURL ECVAM, and adopted under OECD Test Guideline 442E.",
"TM2010-02": "[Validation readiness / evidence package] In May 2012, EURL ECVAM requested clarification regarding technical protocol details and validation readiness. Status: historical. [Access / implementation barriers] The validation of the PALM test method could not be pursued due to unresolved Material Transfer Agreement (MTA) and proprietary licensing issues with the test system owner. Status: terminal bottleneck. [Programme prioritisation / route selection] In June 2013, EURL ECVAM formally notified the submitter that the method would not be prioritised for validation and closed the submission. Status: historical.",
"TM2010-01": "[Validation readiness / evidence package] At the submission stage in 2011–2012, the submission lacked a comprehensive multi-laboratory validation evidence package for regulatory qualification. Status: historical. [Programme prioritisation / route selection] In August 2012, EURL ECVAM formally communicated that the method was not prioritised for validation follow-up, closing the submission track (regulatory preference shifted toward non-radioactive and stably transfected transactivation assays). Status: historical.",
"TM2014-01": "[Validation readiness / evidence package] In December 2018, EURL ECVAM identified gaps during the completeness check of the submission dossier. Status: historical / resolved via international ring trial. [Programme prioritisation / route selection] In April 2019, the submitter formally communicated that the submission would no longer be pursued through EURL ECVAM because the method was directly transferred to the OECD Test Guidelines Programme (WNT approval in April 2019). Status: historical / successfully transferred. [Standardisation / regulatory adoption] Standardisation bottleneck resolved externally: the assay was formally adopted as OECD Test Guideline 249 in June 2021.",
"TM2011-07": "[Validation readiness / evidence package] Pre-submission in 2011–2012 required submission of additional data; upon assessment of the revised pre-submission in February 2013, EURL ECVAM determined that the evidence package and inter-laboratory readiness were not sufficient to progress to formal validation. Status: historical. [Programme prioritisation / route selection] In February 2013, EURL ECVAM formally communicated that the method would not progress further towards validation, as validation resources and priorities were directed towards established KE3 assays (e.g. h-CLAT, MUSST). Status: historical.",
"TM2008-11": "[Validation readiness / evidence package] At the pre-submission stage in 2008, the submission consisted of an early-stage test description without a comprehensive multi-laboratory validation evidence package for protein allergenicity. Status: historical. [Programme prioritisation / route selection] In November 2008, EURL ECVAM formally communicated that the CPA-I test was not prioritised for validation follow-up, closing the submission. Status: historical.",
"TM2011-14": "[Validation readiness / evidence package] At pre-submission in 2011–2012, an overarching testing strategy combining multiple distinct assays lacked a standardized validation evidence package and harmonized integration rules. Status: historical. [Regulatory relevance / integration] Acceptance of in vitro testing strategies as stand-alone replacements for in vivo tests (LLNA/GPMT) required internationally harmonized frameworks for Defined Approaches. Status: historical / resolved via OECD GL 497 (2021). [Programme prioritisation / route selection] EURL ECVAM formally closed the pre-submission pathway in June 2013 because skin sensitisation testing strategies and IATAs were transferred to the international OECD Task Force on Hazard Assessment. Status: historical.",
"TM2008-12": "[Validation readiness / evidence package] In December 2008, EURL ECVAM assessed the informal study report and notified the submitter that the submitted information did not fulfil validation submission requirements; the submitter was asked to clarify scientific issues and complete the official ECVAM submission form. Status: historical / resolved via resubmission (TM2011-01). [Programme prioritisation / route selection] The initial submission was formally closed/stopped in April 2011 because the method was officially resubmitted on the standard EURL ECVAM template under TM2011-01. Status: historical / resolved via resubmission (TM2011-01).",
"TM2006-01": "[Applicability domain] EURL ECVAM and ECHA identified specific domain limitations for the zebrafish embryo test: bulky or high-molecular-weight substances (> 3–4 kDa) show restricted penetration across the embryonic chorion, and immature embryonic xenobiotic metabolism (specifically Phase I CYP enzymes before 48–72 hpf) can result in lower sensitivity to pro-toxicants requiring metabolic activation compared to adult fish. Status: active / managed. [Regulatory relevance / integration] Despite the formal adoption of OECD TG 236 in July 2013, regulatory uptake under REACH has faced substantial integration barriers; ECHA determined in 2016 that OECD TG 236 cannot serve as a direct standalone 1-to-1 replacement for the adult fish test (OECD TG 203) across all standard information requirements, restricting its practical regulatory use primarily to Weight-of-Evidence (WoE) adaptations and tiered testing strategies. Status: active.",
"TM2015-01": "[Validation readiness / evidence package] In May–June 2015, EURL ECVAM assessed the pre-submission (TPF) and formally concluded that the method was not considered for follow-up; the submission lacked a mature multi-laboratory validation evidence package and standardized transferability data across the broad spectrum of claimed matrices. Status: historical. [Programme prioritisation / route selection] EURL ECVAM formally decided not to prioritise the ex vivo hemidiaphragm assay for validation within its institutional work programme, discontinuing the pre-submission pathway in June 2015. Status: historical.",
"TM2014-05": "[Validation readiness / evidence package] In January 2015, EURL ECVAM formally concluded that the pre-submission was not considered for follow-up because the submitted information did not fulfil ECVAM validation criteria; the evidence package lacked standardized multi-laboratory validation and transferability data across the broad spectrum of claimed endpoints. Status: historical / superseded by TM2021-01. [Applicability domain] The submission claimed an overly expansive applicability domain, proposing LUCS as a universal replacement for the 3T3-NRU assay across acute oral toxicity, phototoxicity, and even skin corrosion and irritation, without sufficient supporting domain evidence. Status: historical. [Programme prioritisation / route selection] EURL ECVAM formally decided not to prioritise the method for validation, discontinuing the pre-submission pathway. Status: historical.",
"TM2011-01": "[Validation readiness / evidence package] Following initial evaluation of the pre-submission in July 2011, EURL ECVAM requested clarifications and a revised protocol; the submission lacked sufficient experimental validation data to substantiate the claimed applications across multiple toxicological endpoints. Status: historical / superseded by TM2014-05. [Applicability domain] The DAP technology was proposed with an overly broad applicability domain spanning acute toxicity, chronic toxicity, genotoxicity, and carcinogenicity, but lacked experimental domain characterization beyond preliminary acute cytotoxicity data. Status: historical. [Programme prioritisation / route selection] The submission was formally closed at EURL ECVAM in May 2012 because the submitter withdrew and replaced the method with a subsequent, refined version (TM2014-05, LUCS). Status: historical.",
"TM2010-08": "[Validation readiness / evidence package] At the time of evaluation in 2011–2013, the transcriptomics-based classifier lacked an internationally standardized multi-laboratory validation dossier demonstrating inter-laboratory reproducibility and transferability of the complex microarray and bioinformatic pipeline across GLP environments. Status: historical. [Regulatory relevance / integration] European and international regulatory frameworks for genotoxicity (OECD Test Guidelines, ICH S2(R1), REACH) require apical DNA or chromosomal damage endpoints; in 2013, no regulatory testing framework existed to accept standalone or battery toxicogenomic gene-expression classifiers for regulatory hazard classification. Status: historical. [Programme prioritisation / route selection] In September 2013, following the assessment of the full submission, EURL ECVAM formally concluded that the method was not considered for validation, terminating the submission pathway. Status: historical.",
"TM2014-03": "[Validation readiness / evidence package] As recorded in the EURL ECVAM pre-submission assessment, the 3D human airway epithelial model demonstrated high biological relevance, but lacked an established standardized protocol and clear mathematical data analysis/prediction model for calculating regulatory absorption parameters. Status: historical. [Regulatory relevance / integration] At the time of evaluation in 2014, EURL ECVAM's validation mandate prioritized apical toxicological endpoints over individual pharmacokinetic/ADME screening tools; there was no dedicated regulatory framework within REACH or OECD to integrate standalone respiratory permeability values. Status: historical. [Programme prioritisation / route selection] In December 2014, EURL ECVAM formally concluded that the pre-submission was not prioritised for validation and discontinued the submission pathway, noting it was to be followed up within the broader context of ADME methods. Status: historical.",
"TM2011-04": "[Validation readiness / evidence package] At the pre-submission stage in 2011–2012, the bioaccessibility testing method lacked a standardized, multi-laboratory validated evidence package; the submission proposed broad, multi-fluid testing across powders and massive sheets without standardized inter-laboratory validation data. Status: historical / subsequently addressed in TM2016-02. [Applicability domain] The proposed applicability domain was overly broad and heterogeneous, covering inhalation, ingestion, and dermal artificial fluids for both powders and massive alloy sheets without clear boundaries or domain validation. Status: historical / refined to simulated gastric fluid in later work. [Regulatory relevance / integration] In 2012, EURL ECVAM and European chemical regulators lacked an established regulatory testing framework to validate cell-free bioaccessibility/bioelution screening methods as standalone NAMs for chemical grouping and read-across. Status: historical. [Programme prioritisation / route selection] In August 2012, EURL ECVAM formally evaluated the revised pre-submission and concluded that the method was not considered for follow-up, discontinuing the submission pathway. Status: historical.",
"TM2012-01": "[Validation readiness / evidence package] At the time of the TSAR submission in 2012–2013, the in vitro BINACLE assay required comprehensive international multi-laboratory collaborative validation to demonstrate equivalence and robustness across official medicines control laboratories (OMCLs) and vaccine manufacturers before it could replace the European Pharmacopoeia guinea pig test. Status: active / addressed through EDQM BSP136. [Programme prioritisation / route selection] Formal submission at EURL ECVAM was discontinued in June 2013 because the method was transitioned to the European Directorate for the Quality of Medicines & HealthCare (EDQM) Biological Standardisation Programme (project BSP136: Tetanus Toxoid BINACLE assay), representing the competent European regulatory standardisation route for biologicals and pharmacopoeial monographs. Status: resolved / routed to EDQM.",
"TM2013-01": "[Regulatory relevance/integration] At the time of presubmission in 2013, EURL ECVAM focused validation activities primarily on apical toxicological hazard endpoints rather than individual pharmacokinetic/ADME screening tools. Consequently, the test method did not fit an immediate regulatory testing requirement within European chemical safety regulations (e.g. REACH). Status: historical. [Programme prioritisation/route selection] Following evaluation of the pre-submission in October 2013, EURL ECVAM decided that the method was not prioritised for validation and discontinued the submission pathway, noting it was to be considered in a wider context of ADME methods. Status: historical.",
"TM2007-03": "[Predictive performance] In the EURL ECVAM follow-up validation study and ESAC peer review, the 3T3 NRU assay demonstrated high sensitivity (ca. 95%) and a low false negative rate (ca. 5%), but showed poor specificity (40–44%) and a high false positive rate, over-predicting many non-toxic chemicals as toxic. Furthermore, positive results cannot differentiate among the acute oral toxicity hazard categories (UN GHS / EU CLP Categories 1 to 4). Consequently, the method cannot serve as a standalone replacement for acute oral toxicity testing, but is restricted to identifying non-classified substances within a Weight of Evidence (WoE) or Integrated Testing Strategy (ITS). Status: active / managed under ECHA Guidance R.7a. [Applicability domain] As documented in the EURL ECVAM Recommendation (2013), the method has defined domain limitations: it fails to identify chemicals that act through organ-specific mechanisms absent in fibroblasts (e.g. neurotoxicity, cardiotoxicity) or that require in vivo metabolic activation by cytochrome P450 enzymes; highly volatile and poorly soluble/precipitating chemicals are also outside the reliable applicability domain. Status: active / managed.",
"TM2003-01": "[Predictive performance] In the international NICEATM/ECVAM validation study and ICCVAM peer review, in vitro basal cytotoxicity (IC50 in 3T3 and NHK cells) demonstrated only moderate correlation with rodent oral LD50 values (r² ≈ 0.52–0.58). Because basal cytotoxicity assays cannot account for in vivo toxicokinetics, metabolic activation, or target-organ systemic toxicity, the assay could not accurately predict acute systemic lethal potency or standalone GHS hazard categories. Consequence: the method could not be adopted as a full replacement Test Guideline, but was restricted to estimating starting doses to reduce animal use. Status: active / managed under OECD GD 129. [Applicability domain] As documented in OECD Guidance Document 129 and the ICCVAM evaluation report, the method has defined domain limitations: it fails to reliably predict substances acting via specific mechanisms lacking in basal cell cultures (e.g. neurotoxicity, cardiotoxicity), chemicals requiring in vivo metabolic bioactivation, highly volatile substances, and insoluble chemicals that precipitate in culture medium. Status: active / managed.",
"TM2010-03": "[Applicability domain] As documented in the EURL ECVAM Recommendation (2014) and OECD Test Guideline 442D, the KeratinoSens assay has defined applicability domain restrictions: (a) it relies primarily on cysteine-specific electrophilic modification of the Keap1 sensor protein, meaning chemicals reacting exclusively with other amino acid residues (such as lysine-exclusive haptens) can yield false negatives; (b) the HaCaT-derived cell model possesses limited cytochrome P450 metabolic competence, preventing the detection of pro-haptens requiring enzymatic bioactivation, while slow-oxidising pre-haptens may also go undetected; and (c) chemicals that precipitate in aqueous/DMSO medium, interfere with luminescence, or directly inhibit luciferase enzyme activity are outside the reliable testing domain. Consequence: KeratinoSens cannot be used as a standalone test to predict skin sensitisation hazard or potency, but must be integrated with complementary key event assays (e.g., DPRA, h-CLAT) within Defined Approaches (OECD TG 497). Status: active / largely resolved or managed.",
"TM2014-02": "[Mechanistic relevance] In its presubmission assessment (November 2014), EURL ECVAM explicitly identified a 'lack of mechanistic information' as a primary limitation, because single-biomarker γH2AX detection cannot discriminate clastogenic from aneugenic modes of action or mitotic arrest. Consequence: rejection at presubmission. Later resolution: the developers subsequently resolved this mechanistic gap by adding a second biomarker (pH3) to detect mitotic disruption/aneugenicity, resulting in the evolved dual-biomarker assay resubmitted as TM2017-01. Status: resolved via successor TM2017-01. [Regulatory relevance/integration] EURL ECVAM concluded that 'in the context of the current regulatory testing strategy for genotoxicity assessment, the method would not appear to offer a clear added value' compared to established standard regulatory batteries (such as the in vitro micronucleus test). Consequence: failure to demonstrate regulatory relevance and integration need. Status: historical. [Programme prioritisation/route selection] Directly driven by the regulatory relevance and mechanistic deficiencies, EURL ECVAM formally designated the method as 'Not considered for validation' in November 2014, discontinuing the submission. Status: historical.",
"TM2017-01": "[Validation/readiness] At presubmission in 2017, the emerging dual-biomarker assay protocol and dataset were at an early pre-validation stage, lacking the comprehensive multi-laboratory transferability and validation package required for an immediate full submission and ECVAM-coordinated study. Consequence: EURL ECVAM did not invite a full submission. Later resolution: validation readiness and evidence compilation are being addressed internationally through the OECD Test Guidelines Programme (Project 4.168 Detailed Review Paper). Status: partially resolved / active in OECD. [Programme prioritisation/route selection] Following presubmission assessment in February 2018, EURL ECVAM formally notified the submitter that the method was not prioritised for validation, discontinuing the TSAR submission pathway. Consequence: termination of the ECVAM-coordinated route. Later resolution: the validation and standardisation pathway was redirected to the OECD Test Guidelines Programme (Project 4.168). Status: resolved via route redirection.",
"TM2009-08": "[Regulatory relevance/integration] Following consultation with the EURL ECVAM regulatory network (PARERE - Preliminary Assessment of Regulatory Relevance), regulators concluded that the assay lacked sufficient regulatory need and relevance to replace or supplement established standard in vitro genotoxicity batteries (e.g., ICH S2(R1) / OECD TGs). Consequence: the method did not progress to ESAC independent peer review or regulatory integration. Status: historical. [Programme prioritisation/route selection] As a direct consequence of the negative PARERE regulatory relevance assessment, EURL ECVAM discontinued the submission process in September 2014 prior to peer review. Consequence: termination of the ECVAM validation and adoption pathway. Status: historical.",
"TM2008-09": "[Validation/readiness] In the ECVAM-coordinated Eye Irritation Validation Study (EIVS, 2008–2013), the original generic SkinEthic HCE protocol failed to comply fully with the pre-defined acceptance criteria set by the Validation Management Group. In November 2014, the ESAC concluded that protocol optimisation was necessary, specifically recommending distinct testing protocols for liquid and solid chemicals. This limitation prevented regulatory guideline adoption of TM2008-09 and was subsequently resolved through the optimised resubmission TM2015-03 (SkinEthic HCE EIT), which achieved OECD TG 492 adoption. Status: historical / resolved via resubmission (TM2015-03).",
"TM2011-10": "[Applicability domain] The LuSens assay has documented applicability domain limitations defined in OECD TG 442D: because it relies on the Keap1-Nrf2-ARE pathway, it selectively detects cysteine-reactive electrophiles and may miss chemicals reacting exclusively with lysine; HaCaT cells possess limited metabolic competence (pro-haptens requiring extensive metabolic activation may yield false negatives); and test substances directly inhibiting luciferase or causing optical interference require specific viability/quenching controls. Standalone use is restricted, requiring integration with complementary key event assays within Defined Approaches (OECD GL 497). Status: active/managed.",
"TM2014-06": "[Validation/readiness] The full submission for the Sterlab RHE Skin Irritation Test underwent assessment by EURL ECVAM, requiring additional technical clarifications and data follow-up in December 2015. Although supplementary information was provided in May 2016, the evidence package did not progress to formal EURL ECVAM validation coordination, independent ESAC peer review, or inclusion as a validated reference method in OECD TG 439. Status: active / unresolved (dossier stalled at submission assessment).",
"TM2009-06": "[Applicability domain] The DPRA has documented applicability domain limitations defined in OECD TG 442C and EURL ECVAM validation: because it is an acellular in chemico system without metabolic competence, pro-haptens requiring metabolic activation are not detected (yielding false negatives unless auto-oxidation occurs); highly hydrophobic chemicals (log Kow > 3.5) or insoluble substances can precipitate; and chemicals co-eluting at 220 nm interfere with HPLC-UV peptide quantification (mitigated in part by cysteine-only prediction models). Standalone use is restricted, requiring combination with other key event assays within Defined Approaches (OECD GL 497). Status: active/managed.",
"TM1998-04": "No method-specific bottleneck is supported by the evidence. The ESAC concluded in 1998 that scientifically acceptable in vitro methods are practicably available for all levels of monoclonal antibody production and produce antibodies of equal or superior quality compared to the in vivo ascites method. The spurious applicability domain flag derived from 'Not applicable' in the submission snapshot has been removed. Status: resolved / no active bottleneck.",
"TM2012-04": "[Programme/route selection] The method pre-submission was evaluated by EURL ECVAM, and the submission step was closed because the method was 'no longer considered for follow-up'. The exact scientific or strategic reason for termination is not documented in the available snapshot or TSAR history. Status: historical/stopped.",
"TM2005-02": "[Applicability domain] The EURL ECVAM Recommendation (March 2012) and OECD GD 214 (May 2015) document explicit applicability domain boundaries for the SHE pH 6.7 CTA: the assay is validated for pure chemicals tested in the EURL ECVAM prevalidation study (6 chemicals, predominantly genotoxic carcinogens) and the broader OECD DRP 31 dataset; for chemical classes outside this range, verification with reference chemicals is recommended. OECD GD 214 explicitly states that \"for non-genotoxic carcinogens the added value of the SHE CTA is still debated and more information is required.\" This applicability domain limitation — particularly for non-genotoxic carcinogens and untested chemical classes — was one of the key factors that prevented consensus on a formal OECD Test Guideline at the April 2013 WNT meeting. The domain is operationally managed via the GD 214 framework (use within WoE) but remains an active limitation restricting standalone regulatory use. Status: active/partially managed. [Standardisation/regulatory adoption] The draft OECD Test Guideline for the SHE CTA did not achieve consensus at the April 2013 WNT meeting, which was considered premature by several delegations. In November 2014, the OECD Joint Meeting redirected the process toward a Guidance Document (OECD GD 214, May 2015) rather than a Test Guideline. This represents a documented failure to achieve harmonised regulatory standardisation: a Guidance Document describes test procedures for WoE use but does not constitute adopted international regulatory standard. No OECD Test Guideline for the SHE CTA has been adopted as of September 2026. The key documented impediments were: subjective nature of morphological colony scoring, limited mechanistic understanding, and insufficient data on non-genotoxic carcinogens. Status: active — OECD TG not achieved.",
"TM2005-03": "Available evidence supports no method-specific bottleneck. The prevalidation study was completed successfully with demonstrated transferability and reproducibility (ESAC WG Consensus Report, Feb 2011). EURL ECVAM recommended the assay (Mar 2012). The OECD pathway did not reach consensus for a formal Test Guideline due to concerns about scoring subjectivity and limited mechanistic understanding, but these were addressed through the alternative publication of OECD Guidance Document 214 (May 2015) supporting weight-of-evidence use. No specific bottleneck materially blocked or impeded the method's progression through its lifecycle.",
"TM2008-05": "[Applicability domain] Formal applicability domain limitations restrict the standalone use of the h-CLAT: chemicals with log Kow > 3.5 tend to produce false negative predictions due to limited aqueous bioavailability in cell culture medium, and negative predictions for substances with log Kow > 3.5 are formally considered inconclusive under OECD TG 442E; THP-1 cells have limited metabolic capacity for pro-haptens requiring extensive CYP biotransformation; and strongly autofluorescent chemicals emitting at the FITC wavelength interfere with flow-cytometric acquisition. Consequence: negative predictions are restricted and cannot independently rule out skin sensitisation. Resolution: these limitations are managed within OECD TG 442E and through integration into Defined Approaches (e.g. OECD Guideline 36) alongside peptide-reactivity (KE1) and keratinocyte (KE2) assays. Status: active, managed within the Test Guideline and Defined Approaches.",
"TM2016-08": "No documented bottleneck identified.",
"TM2016-07": "Validation/readiness and regulatory integration: in the TSAR snapshot, EASA validation was still in planning. Subsequent external evidence shows that the multilaboratory validation was completed, with a final NICEATM validation report published in 2025 and a peer-reviewed validation publication in 2026. This resolves the validation-stage gap, while EASA remains positioned as a component for skin-sensitisation IATA/defined approaches rather than as a stand-alone broadly adopted regulatory test guideline. Status: validation bottleneck resolved; regulatory integration remains incomplete.",
"TM2016-06": "Validation/readiness, applicability, product-specific qualification and regulatory adoption: the Brazilian MAT programme required demonstration across additional biological products, medical devices, nanomaterials and non-endotoxin pyrogens. Contemporary BraCVAM evidence identified insufficient product coverage, insufficient evidence for non-endotoxin pyrogen detection and the need for product-class-specific validation. In the TSAR snapshot validation remained ongoing, while adoption into the Brazilian Pharmacopoeia was still at the drafting/translation stage. Status: unresolved in the TSAR snapshot.",
"TM2016-05": "Validation/readiness, predictive performance, standardisation and regulatory integration: independent evaluations concluded that further work was required before HET-CAM's scientific validity could be established for regulatory eye-hazard classification. Heterogeneous protocols/prediction models and insufficient predictive-performance evidence were documented obstacles, prompting a prospective BraCVAM validation study initiated in 2015 to assess reliability and predictive capacity. The TSAR record itself states that use in Brazil was as a screening test rather than for regulatory purposes. Status: unresolved in the TSAR snapshot.",
"TM2016-04": "Validation/readiness and predictive performance (2014–2019): prevalidation identified inadequate sensitivity for solid substances and required protocol optimisation before full validation. Multi-laboratory validation subsequently demonstrated that MCTT HCE™ met the OECD TG 492 reproducibility and predictivity performance standards, followed by OECD validation peer review and inclusion in TG 492. Status: Resolved."
};
